From 0ae8fc7d1c8242da2e47589bfc85c7529723aef5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 17:20:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 201/206] [vm] F178: wrap base_entry instead of returning
 identity for linked (cpu) Shorebird entries

Deserializer::WrapCode's fourth return path (resolved.target == cpu, i.e. a
patch function the linker proved byte-identical to its base counterpart) was
unconditionally returning identity on payload_start, same as the two genuine
miss paths above it. That meant every unchanged function in a patch was still
interpreted by the Simulator instead of running natively, even though the
code already computes a real Sim->CPU wrapper trampoline for this case
(WrapperForRunTarget(resolved.target)) and just discarded it.

The B6 PROT_READ-execution hazard documented on the two miss paths does not
apply here: base_entry resolves into the base app image (normally-executable
memory), not the PROT_READ-only patch payload. The hit path already
RELEASE_ASSERTs payload bytes == base bytes before reaching this return, and
Simulator::DecodeSimulatorToNativeTransition's kRunDartOnCPUCode arm (hardened
since F160/F162, including the stack-floor guard) implements the Sim->CPU
escape this wrapper trampoline would hit.

Changed the final return from EntryPoints(payload_start, identity,
payload_info) to EntryPoints(base_entry, wrapper, payload_info), so a linked
entry's offsets resolve against the base image and its entry points go
through the Sim->CPU wrapper instead of staying interpreted.

Measured (host/simulator build only, no device test): sdk_phase_checklist.py
C4 unaffected before/after (14/14 both ways) -- none of its p1..p9 programs
load through the link-table path, so C4 gives zero coverage of this change;
this is a real gap, not a claim the change is validated end-to-end. A direct
proxy measurement of the effect this unlocks (identical bytecode, simulated
vs native) on this sim build: a 200M-iteration arithmetic loop runs in
33869ms under --use_simulator vs 351ms under --no-use_simulator (~96x),
same result value both ways.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/app_snapshot.cc b/runtime/vm/app_snapshot.cc
index 6cc1262865e..ce5bb36ca5c 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/app_snapshot.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/app_snapshot.cc
@@ -10111,7 +10111,13 @@ Deserializer::EntryPoints Deserializer::WrapCode(CodePtr code,
                reinterpret_cast<const void*>(base_entry),
                static_cast<size_t>(length)) == 0);
 #endif
-  return EntryPoints(payload_start, identity, payload_info);
+  // base_entry lives in the base app image, which is normally-executable
+  // memory -- not the PROT_READ-only patch payload that made identity the
+  // right call on the two misses above. So the B6 hazard (Sim->CPU wrapper
+  // handing the Simulator a PROT_READ address to execute natively) does not
+  // apply here; wrap base_entry instead of defaulting to identity on
+  // payload_start, so an unchanged function actually runs on the CPU.
+  return EntryPoints(base_entry, wrapper, payload_info);
 }
 
 // Shorebird. Their :941. Linking needs both halves: a table saying which patch
